\section{Frechet}{

\code{frechet()} is for a strictly positive response with a heavy right
tail. Its parameters are \code{mu} and \code{nu}. As in brms, \code{mu} is the MEAN,
not the scale: the density is Stan's \code{frechet(nu, s)} with scale
\code{s = mu / gamma(1 - 1 / nu)}, which needs \code{nu > 1}. The default link
of \code{nu} is \code{logm1}, \code{log(nu - 1)}, which keeps it there. Under
\code{link_nu = "identity"} a \code{nu} at or below 1 has no mean, and the
density is \code{NaN} there, as brms's is, in the fitted objective as well
as on doubles.
The variance is finite only for \code{nu > 2}; \code{residuals(type = "pearson")} divides by \code{Inf} where it is not.
}

\section{Generalized extreme value}{

\code{gen_extreme_value()} has a location \code{mu}, a scale \code{sigma} and a
shape \code{xi}. The density is
\verb{exp(-t^(-1/xi)) t^(-1 - 1/xi) / sigma} with
\code{t = 1 + xi (y - mu) / sigma}, and its limit at \code{xi = 0} is the
Gumbel density, which the family evaluates exactly at \code{xi = 0} and
to full precision near it. So \code{bf(y ~ x, xi = 0)} fits a Gumbel
model, as in brms.

The support depends on the parameters: \code{y > mu - sigma / xi} for
\code{xi > 0} and \code{y < mu - sigma / xi} for \code{xi < 0}. Outside the
support the density is 0. Its log falls by \code{1e8} per unit of
\code{t} below 0 rather than jumping to \code{-Inf}, so a row a millionth
outside has a log density below -100 and the optimizer meets a slope
that turns it back, not a wall. The start, \code{xi = 0}, is inside the
support for every data set. For \code{xi > -1} the density goes to 0 at
an end of the support, so a maximum has every row inside it. That is
the region brms's \code{scale_xi()} maps its \code{tmp_xi} onto.

In a \code{\link[=mixture]{mixture()}} a component that does not reach a row adds nothing
to that row's density, which is what a mixture density is. brms's
density is \code{NaN} there, which rejects the parameter value, so a brms
mixture holds each component's support over every row.

The default link of \code{xi} is \code{log1p}, \code{log(1 + xi)}, brms's link
for a predicted \code{xi}, which keeps \code{xi > -1}. \code{link_xi = "identity"}
is the other choice brms allows. Under it, \code{xi <= -1} has no
likelihood, so every link holds \code{xi} above -1. Below -1 the
likelihood is unbounded and has no maximum.

brms parameterizes an \code{xi} without a formula differently. It samples
an unbounded \code{tmp_xi} and scales it into the support the current \code{mu}
and \code{sigma} leave, and it reports \code{xi} from it after the fit. That is
a change of variable, so the maximum of the likelihood is the same
point. A fit does not use it, because its range reaches below
\code{xi = -1}. \code{frmtmb.sample::frm_sample()} does, because there the
support's edge is a wall the sampler meets; it holds the range above
-1 (see \link{frmtmb-gev-xi-chart}).

A fit whose \code{xi} stops within 1e-3 of -1 with the likelihood not
stationary in \code{xi} there warns: its log-likelihood is a supremum, not a
maximum. A maximum inside that band gets a message. For
\verb{-1 < xi < -0.5} the maximum exists but is not regular (Smith, 1985),
so its standard errors do not have their usual meaning; outside the
band no message says so.

The mean, which \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}} returns, is
\code{mu + sigma (gamma(1 - xi) - 1) / xi}, and \verb{mu + sigma * 0.5772...}
(Euler's constant) at \code{xi = 0}. It is infinite for \code{xi >= 1}, and
\code{fitted()} returns \code{Inf} there. brms's \code{posterior_epred()} uses the
same formula and returns \code{NaN} at \code{xi = 0} and \code{xi = 1} and a finite,
wrong number for \code{xi > 1}. The variance is finite only for
\code{xi < 1 / 2}.

brms 2.23.0 marks \code{gen_extreme_value} deprecated and warns when it
builds the Stan code. frmtmb does not repeat the warning: it is about
brms's plans for its own code, not about the model.
}

\section{Discrete Weibull}{

\code{discrete_weibull()} is for a count \verb{0, 1, 2, ...}. Its parameters
are \code{mu} in \verb{(0, 1)} and a positive \code{shape}, with
\code{P(Y >= y) = mu^(y^shape)}, so \code{P(Y = y) = mu^(y^shape) - mu^((y + 1)^shape)}. \code{mu} is not the mean: it is the probability of a
count of at least 1. The mean, which \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}} returns, is the sum
of \code{mu^(y^shape)} over \code{y >= 1}. frmtmb sums it to a relative
precision of about 1e-12, the tail beyond 1000 terms through its
integral. brms's \code{posterior_epred()} stops the sum at the first term
below 0.001, so the two differ where the tail is long.

\code{cens()} and \code{trunc()} follow brms's discrete conventions (see
\code{\link[=frmtmb_family]{frmtmb_family()}}, Censoring a discrete response): a right-censored
count at \code{k} is \code{P(Y > k)}, and \code{trunc(lb = )} reads its bound as
inclusive.
}

\section{Addition terms}{

All three take \code{weights()}, \code{cens()} and \code{trunc()}. brms also takes
\code{mi()} on a \code{frechet} or \code{gen_extreme_value} response; frmtmb takes
\code{mi()} on a gaussian or student response only.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
n <- 200
dd <- data.frame(x = rnorm(n))

# Frechet: mu is the mean
nu <- 4
mu <- exp(1 + 0.3 * dd$x)
dd$yf <- mu / gamma(1 - 1 / nu) * rexp(n)^(-1 / nu)
fit_f <- frm(yf ~ x, family = frechet(), data = dd)
fixef(fit_f)

# generalized extreme value
xi <- 0.2
dd$yg <- 1 + 0.5 * dd$x + 0.8 * (rexp(n)^(-xi) - 1) / xi
fit_g <- frm(bf(yg ~ x, sigma ~ 1), family = gen_extreme_value(),
             data = dd)
fixef(fit_g)

# discrete Weibull counts
q <- plogis(1 + 0.4 * dd$x)
dd$yd <- ceiling((log1p(-runif(n)) / log(q))^(1 / 1.3) - 1)
fit_d <- frm(yd ~ x, family = discrete_weibull(), data = dd)
head(fitted(fit_d))
}
\references{
Smith, R. L. (1985). Maximum likelihood estimation in a class of
nonregular cases. \emph{Biometrika}, 72(1), 67-90.
}
